# Gantry — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Gantry carries a debugging and automation surface, `window.__gantry`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__gantry` as soon as the game
has initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it,
so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. A pose arranges the
game, and the game's own rules run from there exactly as they do in play: an
edit posed through the surface passes the same placement rules a click does,
and a run started through it is the same run the `run` action starts.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{else}}
Gantry carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the
page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `GantryState`, written in the shape of
`update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<GantryState>`,
and returns the next `GantryState`; a reading takes the state the same way and
returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.startRun(s))`, and a reading against the engine's
current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A posed edit passes the same
placement rules a click does, and a run started through the surface is the same
run the `run` action starts. Where the surface's implementation lives under
`src/` is yours to decide; `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type,
`GantryDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic. Gantry uses no
randomness anywhere, and a run advances only on its fixed tick, so the same
structure and the same tape produce the same run, tick for tick, every time.
Two more properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from ticks and input alone,
  independent of the canvas, of the renderer, and of wall-clock time. The
  dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer, and
  whether an edit is accepted is decided from the structure and the site
  alone.
- Silent refusal as data. A pose the rules refuse leaves the state exactly as
  it was, so a scenario reads the outcome of any pose back from `snapshot` and
  `check` rather than from a return value.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath
the game, and the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or
for the overlay. That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the
key bindings `specs/controls.md` fixes, it reports the pointer position in the
stage's logical units along with the press and release edges, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it reports the pointer position in the
stage's logical units, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Gantry's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`GANTRY_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number,
and the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and
plain objects. A position is `{ x, y, z }` in world units, and every angle is
in degrees.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<GantryState>`. A pose returns the next `GantryState`, built from
the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it
read. The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops
the frame loop advancing a running run from the wall clock, so the simulation
moves only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running
itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas
shows the state the most recent tick left.

### `advance(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks, immediately and in order, each the full
tick pipeline `specs/program.md` fixes, followed by a render. `ticks` defaults
to `1`. It applies while a run is in progress and does nothing on other
screens, where nothing ticks.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the
wall clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `reset()`
{{else}}
### `reset(state)`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value:
the title screen with its first item highlighted, site `0` open, every site's
progress cleared and its stored structure and tape empty, the strut tool, no
pending node, an empty history, the camera at its start pose, an idle run, and
`simTime` at `0`. `muted` is deliberately untouched: muting is a player
preference the runtime owns.

Because it restores exactly the declared fields, any state of your own that a
frame depends on is derivable from them.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `openSite(index)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `openSite(state, index)`
{{/if}}

Poses opening site `index`, counted from `0`, exactly as entering it from the
select screen does, locked or not: the build screen, that site's stored
structure and tape, a cleared history, no pending node, and the camera at its
start pose. It is the surface's way onto any site without playing to it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `addMember(a, b, material)`, `removeMember(id)`
### `setRing(corner)`, `clearRing()`
### `addCounterweight(node)`, `removeCounterweight(node)`
{{else}}
### `addMember(state, a, b, material)`, `removeMember(state, id)`
### `setRing(state, corner)`, `clearRing(state)`
### `addCounterweight(state, node)`, `removeCounterweight(state, node)`
{{/if}}

Pose single structure edits on the build screen, entering the same rule
pipeline the build tools feed, so every refusal in `specs/structure.md`
applies unchanged: a refused edit leaves the structure exactly as it was. Each
successful edit pushes history exactly as a click would. `addMember` assigns
the structure's next member id; `removeMember` takes the id to remove. They
apply on the build screen alone.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setStructure(doc)`
{{else}}
### `setStructure(state, doc)`
{{/if}}

Poses a whole structure at once, on the build screen. `doc` is
`{ members, ring, counterweights }`: `members` a list of
`{ a, b, material }`, `ring` a `{ corner }` or `null`, and `counterweights` a
list of nodes. The document is applied by replacing the site's structure with
an empty one and posing the ring, then each member in order, then each
counterweight, through the same rules as above; member ids are assigned in
list order starting at `0`. If any element is refused, the whole pose is
refused and the structure the call found stays exactly as it was. A successful
pose replaces the undo history with the single structure it replaced.

This is the surface's main lever for the crane itself: it puts a chosen
structure on the site without clicking it together.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setProgram(doc)`
{{else}}
### `setProgram(state, doc)`
{{/if}}

Poses a whole tape, on the build or program screen. `doc` is the list of
steps, in the shape `specs/state.md` gives them. Each command must name a real
axis and carry a rate greater than `0` and at most the axis's max rate, and
each move at least one command, exactly as the tape editor enforces; a
document breaking any of that is refused whole, leaving the tape as it was.
Targets are accepted as written and judged at run time
(`specs/program.md`).

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `check()`
{{else}}
### `check(state)`
{{/if}}

A pure reading: the editor's static check, computed on the spot. It reports

```js
{
  issues: ["no-ring", ...],   // the readiness issues, empty when ready
  cost: <number>,
  budget: <number>,
  stable: <boolean>,
  members: [{ id, force, utilization }],
}
```

With any readiness issue, `stable` is `false` and `members` is empty. With a
ready structure it runs the two solves of `specs/statics.md` at the run-start
posture, `slew` `0`, `trolley` `0`, `hoist` `HOIST_START`, the bare hook
hanging at rest, and no motion: `stable` reports whether both solves are
regular, and `members` carries each intact member's static force and
utilization, in member-id order. Nothing breaks and nothing fails during a
check; a utilization above `1` is simply reported.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `startRun()`, `abortRun()`
{{else}}
### `startRun(state)`, `abortRun(state)`
{{/if}}

`startRun` poses the `run` action: the same refusals, the same `run-start`,
and the same first tick as `specs/program.md` states. A refused start leaves
the state as it was; `check` says why it would refuse. `abortRun` poses the
abort, ending a running run with no verdict and returning to the build
screen.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A typical scenario calls `openSite`, `setStructure`, and `setProgram` to pose
the site wanted, `startRun` with `setAutoStep(false)`, then `advance` through
the run, reading verdicts, forces, and poses from `snapshot()` as it goes.
{{else}}
A typical scenario applies `openSite`, `setStructure`, and `setProgram`
through `engine.apply`, applies `startRun`, steps the engine's own clock
through the run, and reads verdicts, forces, and poses from
`debug.snapshot(engine.state)` as it goes.
{{/if}}

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "select" | "build" | "program" | "run" | "results",
  menuIndex: <number>,
  siteIndex: <number>,
  cleared: [<boolean>],            // one per site
  best: [{ cost, time } | null],   // one per site
  tool: "strut" | "cable" | "rail" | "ring" | "counterweight" | "delete",
  pendingNode: { x, y, z } | null,
  camera: { yaw, pitch, dist },
  structure: {
    members: [{ id, a, b, material }],
    ring: { corner } | null,
    counterweights: [{ x, y, z }],
    cost: <number>,
    issues: [<string>],            // the readiness issues, empty when ready
  },
  program: [
    { kind: "move", commands: [{ axis, target, rate }] },
    { kind: "action", action: "attach" | "release" },
  ],
  run: {
    phase: "idle" | "running" | "cleared" | "failed",
    cause: <string> | null,
    tick: <number>,
    time: <number>,                // tick / TICK_HZ, seconds
    speedIndex: <number>,
    stepIndex: <number>,
    axes: {
      slew:    { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      trolley: { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      hoist:   { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      grip:    { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
    },
    bob: { pos: { x, y, z }, vel: { x, y, z } },
    attached: <number> | null,
    loads: [{ phase, pos: { x, y, z }, yaw }],
    forces: [{ id, force, utilization }],
    broken: [<number>],
  },
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen.
`structure` and `program` report the open site's; `run` reports its idle
placeholder whenever no run is in progress, exactly as `specs/state.md` gives
it. `structure.cost` and `structure.issues` are derived from the rules in
`specs/structure.md`, `run.forces` is the latest solve in member-id order, and
`run.loads` is index-aligned with the site's load list. `muted` mirrors the
runtime's mute bit and `simTime` accumulates every update's delta time,
whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least the current screen and site, the
structure's member count, cost, and readiness issue count, the run's phase,
step index, clock, and cause, each axis's value, the bob's position, the
highest current utilization and the broken-member count, and the camera pose.
Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure
read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Gantry's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<GantryState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the
panel, toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
